## ===========================================================================
## THE ROOFTOPS AND BLACK GATE ESTATE — Book 8 (`story.ni:8743-9580`),
## Chapter 6. The post-sewer layer of Books 3 and 4 (the shops' second
## visits, the morning's refusals) lives in `commerce-street.chord` and
## `lords-market.chord`, gated on the chapter (`after jail`).
##
## The change document ("Chapter 6 — The Rooftops and Black Gate Estate",
## 2026-09-01) authorizes Book 8 whole, from daylight on Commerce Street to
## the butler's side door with the letter: the morning's refusals, the shops'
## second visits (stubs), Holstenoffer's back door, the closed alley and the
## gutter, the rooftops and the jump, the roof of the estate, the house's
## three floors, the winch and the chandelier, the letter, the butler's
## catch, the stairwell, the side door. Every line is Gentry's; conversations
## are STUBS under the standing rule.
##
## THE RULINGS (change document, Chapter 6):
##   * EXTENT — the chapter ends at the butler's side door; Bobby's hanging
##     is Chapter 7's, whose row begins when the butler is done with her
##     (`the butler becomes gone`, `secret-letter.story`). That closes the
##     fourth gap the way Chapter 7's ruling did: the hanging starts at the
##     side door, not in the library where the source starts it.
##   * THE STREET — new content, fear without teeth: mercenaries on Commerce
##     Street that morning, seen or heard and ducked, never taking her. ONE
##     placeholder, on Commerce Street's first entry after the sewer
##     (`commerce-street.chord`); the beats and their lines are David's
##     (gap 1).
##   * THE HOUSE — new content, fear without teeth: servants on the stairs,
##     near misses in the hallway. ONE placeholder, on the Second Floor
##     Landing, on her second arrival there while she is still free (the
##     first arrival is the source's own event, Fossville's voice); the beats
##     and their lines are David's (gap 2). The Baron's "within an hour"
##     stays a line, not a clock.
##   * THE LETTER — carries as written: "Jacqueline Toresal", "her".
##   * WHO SEES HER — the butler SEES: "child" and "little thief" while he
##     holds her, then "A bit of advice, girl" at the door — a tell, carried
##     verbatim. Fossville does NOT see: his "fourteen-year-old girl"
##     through the floor is knowledge from the letter, carried verbatim.
##     Germaise and Holstenoffer do not see, Olgan Minor and Dame Sandler do,
##     as Chapter 2 has them; their second-visit lines are stubs.
##
## STUBS TAKING THE SOURCE'S PATH (flagged, per the standing rule's item 4):
## `talk to holstenoffer` after the sewer prints the HO22-HO24 TODO and moves
## her to the Closed Alleyway, as HO24's side effect does — the chapter's one
## way up. GE16 and HO18 fire on entering their shops; DS24 on entering
## Sandler's, DS30 on leaving it (the source halts the going and the quip
## moves her out; the stub lets the going proceed). OM9 is one more option in
## Olgan's tree and is named in his after-sewer TODO.
##
## GAP 3 (the post-jail self-description, `story.ni:1322`) is Chapter 5's
## window and is reported there; this chapter's "You are utterly filthy."
## (`story.ni:1325`) is a `detail` line on Jack (`secret-letter.story`) that
## APPENDS to her description where the source replaces it.
##
## THE CHAPTER TRIGGER, fixed here: Chapter VI's row began "when the player
## visits the Empty Alleyway for the first time", but the alleyway moves her
## on to Commerce Street in the same turn and the chapter plugin reads her
## location after the action (`chapters-plugin.ts:88`), so the row could
## never fire (GH #368). It now begins when the hatch becomes opened — the
## same clause's own first statement. The tree cannot pin the chapter
## packet either (GH #369); the after-jail gates below are its evidence.
##
## NOT CARRIED, with reasons:
##   * `jump east` / `jump to <room>` — no direction slot on a story action;
##     `jump`, `jump over`, `jump across` and `jump to the balcony` are built.
##   * The harp placed in scope from the Audience Area ("You cannot reach the
##     harp from here") — no scope statement.
##   * The butler's catch-all ("Be still, child, or I'll forget my charity")
##     for every other action — no catch-all clause; going, attacking, talk,
##     show and give carry.
##   * "without printing a room description" on the drag to the landing — an
##     authored move narrates its destination (GH #367 notes the order).
##   * The letter's printed name changing from "folded sheet of parchment"
##     to "letter" once read — a name is fixed; it is `the letter` throughout.
##   * The source's second search wording ("Rolled up…" after "Odd — rolled
##     up…") — the letter leaves the chandelier on the first search, so the
##     second form is unreachable.
##   * The chandelier's `raise`/`lower` verbs — capability actions that never
##     reach an entity clause; `lift` (the jail's story action) carries the
##     too-heavy line through a trait.
##   * Going through the closed sliding doors auto-opens them in the source;
##     the platform refuses a closed door, so `open doors` is the player's.
##     The narrow wooden door between the audience area and the closet starts
##     OPEN for the same reason (the source auto-opens it).
##   * "It is fixed in place" for pushing the winch — the winch and its crank
##     share one trait, so `push winch` turns it.
##   * The distant descriptions' "to the [quick best route]" tails (the
##     patio's trickle, the garden's roses, the balcony) — dropped, as the
##     market's were (`secret-letter.story` header, item 1).
##   * The winch's and rope's state-dependent descriptions are examine
##     refusals keyed on the chandelier (a marker in an entity description
##     prints literally, GH #364), as the lanterns' night text is.
##   * The bed's two `[first time]` sentences follow the description on the
##     first look (an ordinal block) instead of sitting inside it.
##   * `pick <flowers>`, bare `shimmy`, `descend the walls`, the facing
##     texts, the sky from the alley, `taste`, `kiss`, the sun, `turn harp`,
##     `sleep` in the bedroom (the story's `sleeping` is Maiden House's).
##   * `push crank`/`pull crank` fall to the platform's scenery refusal.
##   * `give letter to butler` refuses with his line and never marks him as
##     having seen it — a giving clause that speaks still hands the letter
##     over unless it refuses, and a refusal cannot also change his state;
##     `show` is the path that flips him to "pointedly does not look".
## ===========================================================================

## ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
## THE CLOSED ALLEYWAY behind Commerce Street (`story.ni:8772-8830`)
## ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

create the Closed Alleyway
  a room
  aka thin alley, alley, alleyway, closed alley
  up to the Rooftop, one-way
  south is blocked: no-point-going-back
  north is blocked: alley-nowhere-else
  east is blocked: alley-nowhere-else
  west is blocked: alley-nowhere-else
  down is blocked: alley-nowhere-else

  The walls of the alley are so close together you can barely squeeze your
  shoulders through; the rough brick and mortar catches at your clothes. The
  ends of the alley are walled off. There's no way out except back through
  the little door you came in.

