## THE LETTER (`story.ni:9471-9496`) — the ruling: as written, "Jacqueline
## Toresal", "her". Offstage until the chandelier gives it up; `hidden` →
## `revealed` on the find, `read` on the first reading. Examining it from
## the floor picks it up first, as the source does; taking it unread reads
## it, as the source does.

create the letter
  aka secret letter, parchment, sheet, sheet of parchment, folded sheet, folded parchment, folded sheet of parchment, rolled parchment, roll, page, paper, writing, script, duke's letter
  states: hidden, revealed, read

  The parchment is thin and the ink faded, but the writing is still quite
  legible, a strong, graceful script that flows down the page.

  Your Royal Highness, it begins,

  It is with a sad but hopeful heart that I write to you, for I must reveal
  deeds that I am not proud of, and yet perhaps I may thereby put an end to
  an injustice that has gone on far too long.

  My lawful marriage was determined by political expediency rather than by
  the urgings of my own heart, as you well know. My wife was ever a dutiful
  companion, but never truly happy. I do not blame her. For my part, I did
  my best to ensure that she lived a lifestyle fitting for a Duchess, and
  that she wanted for nothing save my love.

  Were this the full extent of my shame, I would have no need to write to
  you, for a loveless marriage is hardly a rare thing in our kingdom.
  However, it is to confess yet a further disgrace that I must pen this
  letter. I was not merely cool in my affections, but an unfaithful husband
  as well. I took a mistress, a woman on whom I bestowed all the tenderness
  I was unable to give my wife. And with this mistress, I had a child.

  I kept the child's existence a secret, as much to spare my wife the
  ignominy as to avoid the political complications that would otherwise
  have ensued. From my private finances I established an orphanage here in
  the city, and paid a tutor from the Royal College to pose as one of the
  headmistresses, to raise and educate the child properly. In secret I have
  sponsored this child for ten years.

  I feel it is past time for this secrecy to end. I have grown ill with a
  mysterious sickness these past months, and I fear that my life will soon
  come to a close. My wife was never able to provide me with a lawful heir,
  and yet I would not see my family name extinguished merely because I had
  not the courage to face up to my failings as a husband.

  Therefore it is my wish that the crown recognize this child, who bears no
  fault for the circumstances of her birth, as my sole and lawful
  beneficiary, to inherit my title and estate in full upon my death, or upon
  her sixteenth birthday should I die before she reaches the age of
  majority. I hereby, by my will and by royal law, and in full possession of
  my faculties, declare my heir:

  Jacqueline Toresal

  Signed and witnessed therewith,

  Lord William, Duke of Toresal

  on the player examining
    phrase you-pick-up-the-letter when not the player has the letter
    phrase letter-text when not the player has the letter
    move the letter to the player when not the player has the letter
  end on

  after the player examining while the letter is revealed
    phrase letter-trembling-hand
    change the letter to read
  end after

  after the player taking while the letter is revealed
    phrase letter-text
    phrase letter-trembling-hand
    change the letter to read
  end after

define phrase you-pick-up-the-letter
  You pick up the letter.
end phrase

## `story.ni:9484`, the reaction — "the same first name as you", the first
## time she reads her own name in another's hand.

define phrase letter-trembling-hand
  Your hand is trembling as you fold the letter. You tell yourself that it's
  a coincidence, just someone who happens to have the same first name as you
  — but in your heart you know it must be true.

  And yet... it poses so many more questions than it answers. The letter is
  addressed to the queen, and dated three years ago, just a few months before
  the Duke's death. Why was it never posted? And what is it doing in Baron
  Fossville's library?

  A noise from the bedroom startles you out of your puzzling. Now that you
  have what you came for, you need to get out of here — fast.
end phrase

## The letter's text again as a phrase, for "Taken." then the reading
## (`story.ni:9490`) and for the pick-up on examine (`story.ni:9494`) — a
## phrase spoken in an `on` clause replaces the action's own report, so the
## intercept must carry the text itself; a clause cannot run an examine.

define phrase letter-text
  The parchment is thin and the ink faded, but the writing is still quite
  legible, a strong, graceful script that flows down the page.

  Your Royal Highness, it begins,

  It is with a sad but hopeful heart that I write to you, for I must reveal
  deeds that I am not proud of, and yet perhaps I may thereby put an end to
  an injustice that has gone on far too long.

  My lawful marriage was determined by political expediency rather than by
  the urgings of my own heart, as you well know. My wife was ever a dutiful
  companion, but never truly happy. I do not blame her. For my part, I did
  my best to ensure that she lived a lifestyle fitting for a Duchess, and
  that she wanted for nothing save my love.

  Were this the full extent of my shame, I would have no need to write to
  you, for a loveless marriage is hardly a rare thing in our kingdom.
  However, it is to confess yet a further disgrace that I must pen this
  letter. I was not merely cool in my affections, but an unfaithful husband
  as well. I took a mistress, a woman on whom I bestowed all the tenderness
  I was unable to give my wife. And with this mistress, I had a child.

  I kept the child's existence a secret, as much to spare my wife the
  ignominy as to avoid the political complications that would otherwise
  have ensued. From my private finances I established an orphanage here in
  the city, and paid a tutor from the Royal College to pose as one of the
  headmistresses, to raise and educate the child properly. In secret I have
  sponsored this child for ten years.

  I feel it is past time for this secrecy to end. I have grown ill with a
  mysterious sickness these past months, and I fear that my life will soon
  come to a close. My wife was never able to provide me with a lawful heir,
  and yet I would not see my family name extinguished merely because I had
  not the courage to face up to my failings as a husband.

  Therefore it is my wish that the crown recognize this child, who bears no
  fault for the circumstances of her birth, as my sole and lawful
  beneficiary, to inherit my title and estate in full upon my death, or upon
  her sixteenth birthday should I die before she reaches the age of
  majority. I hereby, by my will and by royal law, and in full possession of
  my faculties, declare my heir:

  Jacqueline Toresal

  Signed and witnessed therewith,

  Lord William, Duke of Toresal
end phrase

## ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
## THE BUTLER (`story.ni:9502-9571`): the catch, the grip, the stairwell,
## the side door. He SEES her (the ruling): "child", "little thief", and at
## the door, "girl".
## ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

## Three one-turn clocks, one per row of the source's dialogue table: the
## drag into the stairwell (row one), the wait (row two), the side door
## (row three, and the move out). Chapter VII's row watches for `gone`, and
## the hanging (`gallows.chord`) is set in place here, before her first
## street prints — the change document's trigger ruling (a move offstage
## fires no `moves` clause, so the hanging cannot watch the butler itself).

define timer hauling for the butler
end timer

define timer overhearing for the butler
end timer

define timer releasing for the butler
end timer

create the butler
  aka man, servant, tall man, gaunt man, sour man, old man, older man, fossville's butler, uniform, servant's uniform
  a person
  pronouns he
  states: absent, gripping, shown, gone

  The butler is tall and somewhat gaunt, with a heavy brow and a sour,
  twisted cast to his mouth. He looks like he doesn't like kids very much,
  especially when they're somewhere they shouldn't be.

  phrase present:
    The butler grips your arm firmly.

  on the player talking
    select stopping
      phrase butler-hush
    or
      phrase butler-stern-look
    end select
  end on

  on the player showing
    phrase butler-wont-look when the butler is shown
    phrase butler-stares-at-the-letter when the butler is gripping
    change the butler to shown when the butler is gripping
  end on

  on the player giving
    refuse when the butler is shown: butler-wont-look
    refuse butler-stares-at-the-letter
  end on

  on the player attacking
    refuse butler-death-pinch
  end on

  on the player touching
    refuse butler-death-pinch
  end on

  on the player pushing
    refuse butler-death-pinch
  end on

  when hauling expires
    move the butler to the Second Floor Landing
    move the player to the Second Floor Landing
    phrase stairwell-row-one
    start overhearing
  end when

  when overhearing expires
    phrase stairwell-row-two
    start releasing
  end when

  when releasing expires
    phrase stairwell-row-three
    change the butler to gone
    move the butler offstage
    move the gallows to the Lord's Market
    move the crowd of spectators to the East Commerce Street
    move the servants offstage
    change the hanging to gathering
    move the player to the East Commerce Street
  end when

## `story.ni:9506`

define phrase butler-catches-you
  You leave the library in a rush — and run smack into someone coming the
  other way.

  It's all you can do to bite back a scream. You try to duck around the
  person and run, but he grabs your arm and pulls you back. 'Little thief!' a
  voice hisses, 'What are you doing here? What do you have?'

  He turns you around to face him. He's an older man, wearing a servant's
  uniform — Fossville's butler.
end phrase

define phrase butler-grip-firm
  The butler is stronger than he looks, and his grip on your arm is firm.
end phrase

define phrase butler-death-pinch
  He may look old, but this butler has your arm in some kind of death pinch.
  Every time you struggle, he gives it a painful twist.
end phrase

define phrase butler-hush
  'Hush,' the butler snaps, cutting you off. 'I've no great desire to see
  the master inflict his cruelties upon a child, but I'll not lose my
  position over it. Keep your lips sealed if you want to leave this place
  unscathed.'
end phrase

define phrase butler-stern-look
  The butler silences you with a stern look.
end phrase

## Showing him the letter (`story.ni:9535-9541`), once, then he will not
## look — on a state rather than a `stopping` phrase, because a phrase's
## stopping count is kept per call site and show and give are two sites
## (GH #371).

define phrase butler-stares-at-the-letter
  The butler stares intently at the letter in your hand for a long moment.
  His eyes flicker towards the library, then settle back on you. 'They say
  no dark deed goes unpunished,' he mutters. 'I'll not stand between a man
  and the just fruits of his ill-gotten gains. Put that away, child; I never
  saw it, and I don't want to know what's in it.'
end phrase

define phrase butler-wont-look
  The butler pointedly does not look at it.
end phrase

## The table of the butler's dialogue (`story.ni:9563-9571`), one row a turn.
## Fossville does NOT see her: "fourteen-year-old girl" is what the letter
## told him. The butler DOES: "A bit of advice, girl".

define phrase stairwell-row-one
  The butler drags you into the stairwell, then stops, his thin, bald head
  cocked, listening. From downstairs you hear more voices.

  '...difficult is it to keep one half-witted street urchin locked in a cage
  for a single night?' That's the Baron again, you realize. 'Because it is
  evidently far more difficult than I imagined. Perhaps I am not paying you
  enough for your heroic efforts so far?'

  You hear a low, mumbled response.

  'No, I daresay you don't,' snaps the Baron, his voice acid with sarcasm.
  'I cannot express how galling it is that I still require your services;
  were it otherwise, I would sooner turn you all over to the Lord's Guards
  and let them deal with you like the useless curs you are.'

  The butler glances at you, one bushy eyebrow raised. You're beginning to
  suspect that Baron Fossville is not particularly well liked by his
  servants.
end phrase

define phrase stairwell-row-two
  'Gather your men at Lord's Market,' says Fossville. 'We'll deal with the
  spy first. Once that business is finished, we pay a visit to the Widows.
  Though I suppose I should warn you — you may encounter several unarmed,
  elderly women there. Let us hope they pose no greater challenge to your
  mercenaries than a fourteen-year-old girl.'

  The second voice mumbles another response.

  'See that you do. I will be there shortly.'

  You hear footsteps, and then a heavy door slamming.

  The butler holds his hand up, palm out. Wait.
end phrase

define phrase stairwell-row-three
  Several agonizing minutes later, you hear the door slam a second time. The
  butler waits a few minutes more, then drags you downstairs. He takes you
  through the kitchens to a side door, and shoves you unceremoniously out
  onto the street.

  'Go now, and don't come back,' he says. 'Next time, it won't be me that
  catches you. And if it is me, it'll go that much worse for you. A bit of
  advice, girl,' he adds, just before turning away. 'Keep yourself out of
  the Baron's way. He's not one to let a trifling of blood get in between
  him and his ambitions.'

  He slams the door.
end phrase
